From cbe83aa3ead4d334b04f567a748f78b81a48c484 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 20:44:38 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] F-06: measure the GPU tier on one binary, and count the
 placement the prompt claim rested on

Two things in this section were asserted rather than measured. Both are now
measured, and both assertions were wrong in the direction that flattered us.

THE GPU COMPARISON WAS CROSS-BINARY. The text put the Vulkan tier at "roughly
4x faster on both axes" by setting an external record for another project's
binary (pp512 356.43 / tg128 28.15) against our own 85.84 / 6.69 -- exactly the
comparison this project forbids itself, and for a reason: a prior cross-session
comparison of that shape manufactured a 24% regression that did not exist.

Re-measured, same binary, same model, same 519-token prompt, three interleaved
replicates: GPU 327.38 prompt / 34.40 decode, CPU floor 58.15 / 1.88, CPU dual
85.62 / 6.56. The GPU is 3.8x the post-repack CPU tier on prompt and 5.2x on
decode -- not 4x on both. The two arms with published values were re-run as the
run's own known-answer control and reproduced within 6%, which is what makes the
third arm worth anything. Sample counts are uneven (prompt n=6, decode n=3-4)
and stated as measured rather than rounded up.

No prompt comparison against the external 356.43 is drawn even now: that is a
pp512 synthetic and ours is a real 519-token prefill. Our Vulkan decode of 34.40
does exceed their 28.15, and that is offered as an observation, not a result.

THE PROMPT MECHANISM WAS HALF WRONG. The claim was that the same ops stay
CPU-side in both modes and that "on an 8B model that CPU share is large enough
to matter where on the 0.5B g64 models it was not". A split COUNT is not a
placement, so the placements were counted with GGML_SCHED_DEBUG=2 -- counting,
not timing, so a busy box cannot move it.

Placement is confirmed identical between mode 0 and mode 2 across all 24 graph
dumps: dual residency moves no work, which F-09's design requires and had only
asserted. But the CPU share is 4 of 253 (1.6%), and 0 of 253 in the ubatch that
offloads everything -- SMALLER than the 0.5B's 4 of 169 (2.4%), so the stated
reason is false on its own terms.

What is true is better than the guess. The four CPU-resident MUL_MATs are, by
name, ffn_gate-35, ffn_up-35, ffn_out-35 and result_output: the final layer's
whole FFN plus the output head, three of them among the largest matmuls in the
model. A 1.6% share by count is not a 1.6% share by cost. Per-op timing is
still owed and is named as owed -- identifying the four does not prove they
account for the whole +45.3%.

The --no-op-offload arms are the negative control and pass at 100% CPU on every
block, which is what makes the 1.6% a finding rather than a parser artefact.

Origin: lane-110G missions 1a and 1d.
Measured-effect: RIG-A, llama-server, Ternary-Bonsai-8B-Q2_0.g128.gguf, 519-token
  prefill, n_predict 128, three interleaved replicates, one binary. GPU (-ngl 99)
  327.38 prompt / 34.40 decode; CPU floor 58.15 / 1.88; CPU dual 85.62 / 6.56.
  The two arms with published values reproduced within 6% as the run's own
  known-answer control. Placement counted separately with GGML_SCHED_DEBUG=2:
  mode 2 identical to mode 0 across all 24 graph dumps, CPU share 4 of 253.
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
